\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Algebraic extension versus topological cover}

\p{The extensions in \ref{fcap-001D} and \ref{fcap-001F} are exact
sequences of abstract groups. They record a surjective homomorphism and its
two-element kernel. No topology on the groups is used.}

\p{For real quadratic spaces of arbitrary signature, Gallier proves that the
two maps

##{\Pin(p,q)\longrightarrow O(p,q),\qquad
\Spin(p,q)\longrightarrow SO(p,q)}

are topological double covers
\citet{Section 1.8, Proposition 1.21, pp. 39--40}{gallier2014clifford}.}

\p{Gallier then treats the compact groups: #{\Spin(n)} is path-connected for
#{n\geq 2}, and for #{n\geq 3} it is simply connected and hence the universal
cover of #{SO(n)} \citet{Section 1.8, pp. 40--42}{gallier2014clifford}. These
claims are not being extended here to arbitrary signature. Kostant identifies
the differential of the complex Spin cover with the
quadratic Clifford Lie algebra \citet{Section 2.4, Theorem 8, pp. 286--287}{kostant1997clifford}.
Those conclusions require topological or Lie-theoretic structure not supplied
by an abstract group extension. They are not asserted by the four cards above.}
